==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|releeve||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From Late {{inh|en|enm|releven}}, from {{der|en|fro|relever}}, specifically from the conjugated forms such as {{m|fro|relieve|(jeo) relieve|I lift up}}, and its source, {{der|en|la|relevo||to lift up, lighten, relieve, alleviate}}, combined form of {{m|la|re-||back}} + {{m|la|levo||to lift}}. {{doublet|en|relevate}}. Compare {{m|en|levant}}, {{m|en|levity}}, etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈliːv/|a=}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-relieve.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈliv/|a=US,CA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-relieve.ogg|a=Southern California}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈliːv/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹəˈliːv/|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːv|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ease]] (a person, person's thoughts etc.) from mental [[distress]]; to stop (someone) feeling [[anxious]] or worried, to alleviate the distress of. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I was greatly '''relieved''' by the jury's verdict.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Then we relapsed into a discomfited silence, and wished we were anywhere else. But Miss Thorn '''relieved''' the situation by laughing aloud, and with such a hearty enjoyment that instead of getting angry and more mortified we began to laugh ourselves, and instantly felt better.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ease]] (someone, a part of the body etc.) or give [[relief]] from physical pain or discomfort. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|liss}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[alleviate]] (pain, distress, mental discomfort etc.). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To provide [[comfort]] or [[assistance]] to (someone in need, especially in poverty). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[lift up]]; to [[raise]] again. {{defdate|15th–17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} To [[raise]] (someone) out of danger or ''from'' (a specified difficulty etc.). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} To free (someone) from [[debt]] or legal obligations; to give legal relief to. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|This shall not '''relieve''' either Party of any obligations.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring [[military]] help to (a besieged town); to lift the siege on. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=w:John H. Makin; w:Norman J. Ornstein|title=Debt and Taxes: How America Got into Its Budget Mess and What We Can Do about It|location=New York, NY|publisher=w:Times Books|isbn=081292312X|page={{gbooks|8OWr-C2SqDoC|52|relieve}}|passage=In 1574, the duke of Alva laid siege to Leiden to gain control of Holland's most beautiful and prosperous city. To '''relieve''' the siege, William of Orange and his followers opened the city's protective dikes to flush out—literally—the surrounding Spanish forces.}}
# To [[release]] (someone) from or of a [[difficulty]], unwanted task, responsibility etc. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Eyes of Max Carrados|passage=Carrados was carrying a small leather case which he allowed Loudham to '''relieve''' him of, together with his hat and gloves.}}
# {{lb|en|originally|military}} To free (someone) from their post, task etc. by taking their place. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} To make (something) [[stand out]]; to make [[prominent]], bring into [[relief]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1819|author=w:Lord Byron|title={{w|Don Juan (Byron)|Don Juan}}|section=III.76
|passage=The henna should be deeply dyed to make / The skin '''relieved''' appear more fairly fair {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927|author=w:Countee Cullen|title=From the Dark Tower
|passage=The night whose sable breast '''relieves''' the stark / White stars is no less lovely being dark}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|euphemistic}} To [[urinate]] or [[defecate]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:defecate|Thesaurus:urinate}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|euphemistic}} To ease one's own [[desire]] to [[orgasm]], often through [[masturbation]] to orgasm.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:masturbate}}

